    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. can u expand a bit on the management side? specifically if there are any hidden costs or weird complexities in the way mutual funds are managed compared to ETFs that i should warn the client about?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When it comes to management, the active management in some mutual funds can lead to higher operating expenses, which are essentially fees that eat into your returns. These fees, often called expense ratios, can feel like a hidden cost, especially if the fund manager is just chasing performance without much added value. Some actively managed mutual funds might also engage in more frequent trading, which can generate its own transaction costs. For comparison, passive ETFs generally have very low expense ratios because they are just mirroring an index, which is a simpler, less complex operation.",
      "source": "target",
